WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.724 --> 00:00:06.953
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.953 --> 00:00:09.704
I'm Jesper Sorensen and I'm a professor
of organizational behavior at

00:00:09.704 --> 00:00:11.760
the Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.500
I teach a course called crafting and
leading strategy.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.052
Here are five key
takeaways from that course.

00:00:17.052 --> 00:00:21.404
[MUSIC]

00:00:21.404 --> 00:00:25.084
We often think of strategists as
people who articulate grand visions,

00:00:25.084 --> 00:00:28.980
draw up a blueprint, and
then tell their teams to make it happen.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.270
That's wrong.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.947
A strategy is a continually
evolving course of action,

00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:36.450
not a monument to your genius.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.765
The difference between architecture and

00:00:38.765 --> 00:00:42.931
strategy is that while the ground
underneath a building rarely changes and

00:00:42.931 --> 00:00:47.140
unforeseen ways, everything in
your world is subject to change.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.594
And that means that you cannot simply do a
bunch of analysis, come up with an answer,

00:00:51.594 --> 00:00:55.650
and then give people marching orders and
wait to see the results.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.920
You need to think about
your strategy every day.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.149
It is not a set it and
forget it kind of thing.

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:04.601
[MUSIC]

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:08.257
Executives around the world lack
confidence in their company's

00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:11.490
strategies and
in their own strategy skills.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.830
One big reason for this is that
they equate strategy with planning.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:21.830
They believe that doing strategy requires
uniquely rare expertise and insights.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:26.162
But as Glen Carol and
I argue in our book Making Great Strategy,

00:01:26.162 --> 00:01:31.969
the core of any sustainably successful
strategy is a logically coherent argument,

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:36.660
a story about how actions and
investments lead to success.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.320
We all engage in this kind of
reasoning in our daily lives.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.681
But too often when it comes to strategy,

00:01:42.681 --> 00:01:47.120
we forget to apply our reasoning
skills in a disciplined way.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.143
Nobody knows your organization and
your market better than you.

00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:54.043
All that stands between you and
a great strategy

00:01:54.043 --> 00:01:58.987
is a disciplined reasoning that surfaces
the most critical drivers of success.

00:01:58.987 --> 00:02:02.512
[MUSIC]

00:02:02.512 --> 00:02:06.503
Some years ago I interviewed executives
at a large Silicon Valley company

00:02:06.503 --> 00:02:11.240
to understand the challenges they were
facing with strategy and execution.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:16.520
One executives said to me, the problem is
that we talk about the what not the why.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.649
In other words, company leaders
would issue a to do list of sorts,

00:02:20.649 --> 00:02:26.426
grow market share by 5%, cut costs by 10%,
invest more resources in marketing, etc.

00:02:26.426 --> 00:02:30.849
But they were never clearly told
how these things fit together.

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:35.238
What the underlying logic of
action was supposed to be.

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:40.490
As a leader with a coherent strategy,
you have a method to your madness.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.520
Your strategy is your unique
way of connecting the dots.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.028
But if you only give people a to do list,

00:02:47.028 --> 00:02:50.071
you force them to connect
the dots on their own.

00:02:50.071 --> 00:02:54.342
It can get much more likely that they
will do something you do not want.

00:02:54.342 --> 00:02:58.992
Instead, whenever you ask someone
to accomplish a specific objective,

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:02.442
always help them see how it
fits into the bigger story,

00:03:02.442 --> 00:03:05.077
how it fits in the logic of your strategy.

00:03:05.077 --> 00:03:08.730
[MUSIC]

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.643
As strategic leaders, we continually
strive to improve our strategy,

00:03:12.643 --> 00:03:16.314
to find ways to improve our
organization's economic prosperity.

00:03:16.314 --> 00:03:18.840
But how can we best do this?

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.561
The starting point is to embrace the idea
that your strategy is your theory of

00:03:23.561 --> 00:03:27.323
success, or your belief about
how causes lead to effects and

00:03:27.323 --> 00:03:29.850
how inputs lead to outputs.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.270
But these theories only exist in the mind.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.850
How do we know they are true or
worth pursuing?

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.373
Well, we can start by recognizing that
every time we act in accordance with our

00:03:40.373 --> 00:03:43.260
strategy we are generating evidence.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.914
Did our decision lead to
the consequences we expected?

00:03:46.914 --> 00:03:50.660
If they did, we can feel more confident in
our strategy and sleep better at night,

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:51.651
at least for a while.

00:03:51.651 --> 00:03:56.300
And if they did not,
we may need to rethink our strategy.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.769
Fundamentally, every strategist should
think like a scientist, using evidence and

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:02.801
data to improve their understanding.

00:04:02.801 --> 00:04:06.894
[MUSIC]

00:04:06.894 --> 00:04:11.560
I like to ask students how they would
know whether they have a good strategy.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:16.264
They'll debate this for a long time
talking about profit and growth and KPIs,

00:04:16.264 --> 00:04:18.750
but it's a trick question.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:24.342
The truth is you can never really be sure
that your success is due to your actions,

00:04:24.342 --> 00:04:27.890
as opposed to factors beyond
your control or luck.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:34.079
This messiness and uncertainty frustrates
many people when it comes to strategy,

00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:37.180
but here's a different
way to think about it.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:42.420
The uncertainty is the price
of discovery and greatness.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:47.350
Our strategy is an expression
of how we think the world works.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:52.285
But we should also embrace the idea that
doing strategy is a learning process,

00:04:52.285 --> 00:04:57.820
that is an opportunity for us to
understand more about how the world works.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:02.162
If we can embrace this uncertainty,

00:05:02.162 --> 00:05:09.592
we'll be in a better position to
make the world a better place.

00:05:09.592 --> 00:05:15.068
[MUSIC]

00:05:15.068 --> 00:05:19.600
I wrote this book called
Making Great Strategy which my

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.942
adult kids love to make fun
of especially the title.

00:05:23.942 --> 00:05:28.322
And so every time we now try to do
something like go to a restaurant or

00:05:28.322 --> 00:05:33.095
talk about a problem in their job or
anything like that, they're like,

00:05:33.095 --> 00:05:37.008
well, it's because they didn't
make a great strategy and

00:05:37.008 --> 00:05:40.324
then they laugh laboriously,
and so on so forth.

00:05:40.324 --> 00:05:43.313
And by the way, I think only one
of them has read the book, so.

00:05:43.313 --> 00:05:53.313
[MUSIC]

